{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790706090,"nonce":"4L+cxc8Gm1WAA3ao4XIor63tYUY=","title":"Preserve reflective type values through Dynamic arrays","message":"TestReflect could not compile because __unprotect__(s) was treated as unsupported target code, even though this intrinsic preserves its argument. After allowing it to run, a smaller probe showed that `Type.getClassName(String)` and `Type.resolveClass(\"String\")` failed because the runtime had no class entry for the compiler's stable String class ID. A class token read from `Array\u003cDynamic\u003e` is a DynamicValue box, while Type query functions were using the box address as the type ID. Finally, the MIR for a static `Array\u003cDynamic\u003e` of `[null, Int, String, Bool, Float, Array, Date]` omitted the three primitive abstract type values entirely, shifting all later indices.\n\nTreat __unprotect__ as its operand in typing; register String under its stable class ID; decode boxed type tokens at Type query boundaries; and lower Bool, Int and Float type values to their runtime IDs. The authored TypeTokensInDynamicArrays regression checks array length, boxed String and Array names, and String resolution. All 77 authored regressions pass. The full tiered report has no scored pass regression; TestReflect advances from COMPILE_FAIL to WRONG_ANSWER and remains open for further reflection work.","files":null}]}